{"reply":"The note was kept by Sol Arven at Nacre Ridge in April 2017 and concerns 30 adult silverfold moths, an invented species, housed individually in mesh enclosures. The moths had been collected from one sheltered grove, and their resting positions were recorded before any screen was moved. In each enclosure, a cloth screen shaded one side. Staff moved that screen to the opposite side, waited 24 minutes, and then recorded the moth’s resting position again. The support branches were left in place, the enclosures were not turned, and the same recording sheet was used before and after each move. A blank entry meant the observer could not locate the animal, not that the animal was recorded as having failed to move. The note gives no illumination instrument readings. After the screen move, 21 moths were recorded resting on the newly shaded side, while 9 remained at their recorded original positions. No individual movement path was recorded, so the note could not determine whether a moth moved directly to the shaded side or tried several positions first. The record also does not classify the nonresponders as a different variety.

The follow-up occurred the next afternoon: 4 of the responding moths received a reverse screen move, and all 4 were found on the newly shaded side at the second observation. Branch positions were unchanged. The note says this short follow-up shows repeatable position changes in that selected subset, but it does not estimate a response rate for an independent sample.

The note states several limits. It treats shelter from light only as a working explanation, not an isolated mechanism, because the screen might also have changed air movement or local temperature. The observers did not run a screen-free control, did not compare juveniles with adults, and performed no sensory-organ examination. The note contains no sex breakdown, enclosure dimensions, or thermometer specification, and it does not report behavior after release. Its conclusion is restricted to the recorded screen moves and cannot establish a seasonal habitat preference. It concerns resting behavior inside enclosures, not abundance or migration of moths outside the station."}

{"reply":"(1) My source does not provide verbatim wording for the note's opening or final sentences, so I cannot quote them exactly. Paraphrased opening: Sol Arven's April 2017 field-station note at Nacre Ridge concerns 30 individually housed adult silverfold moths and records their resting positions before and after a cloth shade screen is moved. Paraphrased final point: the note's conclusion is limited to the recorded screen moves and does not establish a seasonal habitat preference.

(2) The explicit numbers in the supplied note are: 2017 (April 2017); 30 (adult moths); 1 (one sheltered grove); 1 (one side of each enclosure); 24 (minutes waited); 21 (moths resting on the newly shaded side after the move); 9 (moths remaining at their original recorded positions); 4 (responding moths given the reverse move); 4 (all four found on the newly shaded side). The note also uses the ordinals 'first' and 'second' for recordings or observations, but these are not additional counted quantities.

(3) The note does not give exact calendar dates for the main observation or the follow-up; it only places the main note in April 2017 and the follow-up on the next afternoon. It does not give an exact time of day for the main observation, and for the follow-up only 'afternoon' is implied. It does not state the number of observers. It does not describe the cloth screen's material, color, or dimensions. It does not give a more precise enclosure location beyond the Nacre Ridge field-station context and individual mesh enclosures; the one sheltered grove is the collection source, not a stated enclosure-location detail. It also does not state how long the moths had been housed before the first recording."}
